# {{.project_name}}

A data product on Databricks. [dlt](https://dlthub.com) lands the data{{ if eq .include_dbt "yes" }}, [dbt](https://getdbt.com)
shapes it{{ end }}, one job runs {{ if eq .include_dbt "yes" }}both{{ else }}it{{ end }}, and the schemas are the bundle's. Made with
[vierlingh](https://github.com/kostavo-oss/vierlingh), run with
[leeghwater](https://github.com/kostavo-oss/leeghwater){{ if eq .include_lely "yes" }}, deployed with
[lely](https://github.com/kostavo-oss/lely){{ end }}{{ if eq .include_caland "yes" }}, its secrets kept with
[caland](https://github.com/kostavo-oss/caland){{ end }}.

```
src/{{.package_name}}/pipelines/   the dlt pipelines: one function each, marked @pipeline
{{- if eq .include_dbt "yes" }}
dbt/models/                 the dbt models, reading raw through source('raw', ...)
{{- end }}
resources/schemas.yml       raw{{ if eq .include_dbt "yes" }}, raw_staging, silver{{ else }} and raw_staging{{ end }} — the bundle makes them, the job gets their names
resources/*.job.yml         the job: ingest{{ if eq .include_dbt "yes" }} → transform{{ end }}
mise.toml                   every task; `mise tasks` lists them
```

## Here, with nothing but Python

```sh
mise install            # python, uv, the Databricks CLI{{ if eq .include_lely "yes" }}, lely{{ end }}{{ if eq .include_caland "yes" }}, caland{{ end }}
uv sync
mise run dev            # the example pipeline, into example.duckdb
mise run check          # lint, format, the pipelines import{{ if eq .include_dbt "yes" }}, dbt parses{{ end }}
```

`uv run ingest list` names the pipelines; `uv run ingest run example --count 3` runs one
with an option. dlt keeps a pipeline's state under `~/.dlt/pipelines/<name>/`, so to start
over, delete that folder and the `.duckdb` file.

## On your workspace: the dev target

Copy `mise.local.toml.example` to `mise.local.toml` and fill in your profile from
`~/.databrickscfg`, your warehouse and the catalog. Then:

```sh
mise run deploy         # the schemas, the job and the wheel, prefixed with your name
mise run job            # the job, on the workspace
mise run ingest -- example    # a pipeline from here, into the dev target's schemas
{{- if eq .include_dbt "yes" }}
mise run transform      # dbt from here, against the same schemas, with the CLI's sign-in
{{- end }}
mise run doctor         # what a run from here would use, checked
```

In development mode every schema and the job carry a prefix with your name, so two people
on one workspace never meet. The job is told the deployed names: nothing in the code knows
them.

{{- if eq .include_lely "yes" }}

## Deploying for real: lely

`mise run plan` shows what a deploy would change and changes nothing; `mise run apply`
does it after you have looked; `mise run destroy` takes the target down again. On GitHub
the same happens on every pull request (`.github/workflows/plan.yml` posts the plan as a
comment) and on merge (`apply.yml` applies the plan that was reviewed, not a new one). The
repository needs `DATABRICKS_HOST` and `WAREHOUSE_ID` as variables and `DATABRICKS_TOKEN`
as a secret.

`databricks bundle deploy -t prod` works without lely; lely adds the plan and the review.
{{- end }}

## Secrets

A source asks dlt for a secret: `api_key: str = dlt.secrets.value`. dlt looks in the
environment, then in `.dlt/secrets.toml` on your laptop, then in the secret scope the app
names, under dlt's path joined with `-`:

```sh
databricks secrets create-scope {{ if .secret_scope }}{{.secret_scope}}{{ else }}<scope>{{ end }}
databricks secrets put-secret {{ if .secret_scope }}{{.secret_scope}}{{ else }}<scope>{{ end }} sources-example-api_key
```
{{- if eq .include_caland "yes" }}

`mise run secrets` opens caland on this bundle's workspace: the scopes, their secrets and
who may read them, in a page.
{{- end }}
{{- if not .secret_scope }}

The app names no scope yet: give one in `src/{{.package_name}}/cli.py`
(`App(..., secret_scopes=["<scope>"])`) and pass it in the job (`--secret-scope`).
{{- end }}

## Add a pipeline

A function marked `@pipeline` in `src/{{.package_name}}/pipelines/`; its parameters become
options of `ingest run <name>`. It makes its dlt pipeline with
`leeghwater.create_pipeline(...)` and runs it with `leeghwater.run(...)` — never with
`dlt.pipeline()`: where a run loads is the run's to say. Add a task for it in
`resources/{{.project_name}}.job.yml` with the options the example task passes.
{{- if eq .include_dbt "yes" }}

## Add a model

A `.sql` file in `dbt/models/`, reading raw through `source('raw', '<table>')` (add the
table to `dbt/models/sources.yml`). `mise run check` parses it, `mise run transform` builds
it against the dev target, and the job's `transform` task builds it after `ingest`.
{{- end }}

